media lives… backend a directory on disk <code>offsite_backup.media.filesystem.DirectorySource</code> <code>ROOT</code>, default <code>settings.MEDIA_ROOT</code> a Docker volume mounted into the container that runs the backup <code>offsite_backup.media.filesystem.DirectorySource</code> inside the container a volume is a directory an S3 bucket (any S3-compatible server) <code>offsite_backup.media.s3.S3Source</code> <code>[s3]</code> extra; <code>BUCKET</code>, <code>PREFIX</code> nowhere leave <code>MEDIA</code> out DirectoryS3 bucket <pre><code>'MEDIA': [
    {
        'BACKEND': 'offsite_backup.media.filesystem.DirectorySource',
        'ROOT': '/app/media',
        'EXCLUDE': ['cache/*', '*/thumbs/*'],
    },
],
</code></pre> <p>Regular files under <code>ROOT</code>, in sorted order; names inside the tar are relative to <code>ROOT</code>. Symbolic links are not followed. A file that cannot be read is logged (<code>Media file not readable; left out</code>) and left out rather than failing the night. A <code>ROOT</code> that does not exist is <code>W005</code> — a missing mount is worth knowing at start, not at 03:00.</p> <pre><code>'MEDIA': [
    {
        'BACKEND': 'offsite_backup.media.s3.S3Source',
        'ENDPOINT_URL': os.environ.get('MEDIA_S3_ENDPOINT', ''),
        'BUCKET': os.environ.get('MEDIA_S3_BUCKET', ''),
        'PREFIX': 'private/media/',
        'ACCESS_KEY': os.environ.get('MEDIA_S3_ACCESS_KEY', ''),
        'SECRET_KEY': os.environ.get('MEDIA_S3_SECRET_KEY', ''),
    },
],
</code></pre> <p>Every object under <code>PREFIX</code>, in key order, streamed straight from the bucket into the tar — nothing is staged on disk but the sealed volume. The object's full key is its name inside the archive. The source has its own credentials: a read-only key for the media bucket, not the backup store's.</p>
